    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "annot-i80wwpmmxrrb",
      "x": 60,
      "y": 870,
      "width": 698.4,
      "height": 126,
      "text": "AS-BUILT 2026-07-11 (/apps embed allowlist): the served-app plane is SPLIT for framing. The\nembeddable app documents (/apps/*) answer CSP frame-ancestors 'self' + the configured dashboard\norigins (EKOA_DASHBOARD_ORIGINS csv -> EKOA_APP_ORIGIN -> dev localhost:3000; invalid entries\ndropped, never widened) and carry NO X-Frame-Options (XFO cannot express an allowlist; legacy\nXFO-only engines fail closed). Every other served path keeps frame-ancestors 'self' + SAMEORIGIN;\nthe JSON API surface keeps frame-ancestors 'none' + DENY. This closes the cross-origin dashboard\npreview-iframe block (findings: apps-embed-frame-headers / the F16-F28 served-app journey gap).",
      "originalText": "AS-BUILT 2026-07-11 (/apps embed allowlist): the served-app plane is SPLIT for framing. The\nembeddable app documents (/apps/*) answer CSP frame-ancestors 'self' + the configured dashboard\norigins (EKOA_DASHBOARD_ORIGINS csv -> EKOA_APP_ORIGIN -> dev localhost:3000; invalid entries\ndropped, never widened) and carry NO X-Frame-Options (XFO cannot express an allowlist; legacy\nXFO-only engines fail closed). Every other served path keeps frame-ancestors 'self' + SAMEORIGIN;\nthe JSON API surface keeps frame-ancestors 'none' + DENY. This closes the cross-origin dashboard\npreview-iframe block (findings: apps-embed-frame-headers / the F16-F28 served-app journey gap).",
      "fontSize": 14,
      "fontFamily": 3,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "strokeColor": "#b45309",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "angle": 0,
      "seed": 1419136668,
      "version": 1,
      "versionNonce": 1335640196,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "groupIds": [],
      "boundElements": null,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "containerId": null,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "rawText": "AS-BUILT 2026-07-11 (/apps embed allowlist): the served-app plane is SPLIT for framing. The\nembeddable app documents (/apps/*) answer CSP frame-ancestors 'self' + the configured dashboard\norigins (EKOA_DASHBOARD_ORIGINS csv -> EKOA_APP_ORIGIN -> dev localhost:3000; invalid entries\ndropped, never widened) and carry NO X-Frame-Options (XFO cannot express an allowlist; legacy\nXFO-only engines fail closed). Every other served path keeps frame-ancestors 'self' + SAMEORIGIN;\nthe JSON API surface keeps frame-ancestors 'none' + DENY. This closes the cross-origin dashboard\npreview-iframe block (findings: apps-embed-frame-headers / the F16-F28 served-app journey gap)."
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "annot-j2j3-20260728",
      "x": 60,
      "y": 1020,
      "width": 900.0,
      "height": 455.0,
      "text": "AS-BUILT 2026-07-28 (J-2/J-3: the connect chain and the one frame that carries a value).\n\nCONNECT is now single-use. A bridge token carries a jti and the connect path SPENDS it exactly\nonce (bridge/connect-nonce.ts). This was not merely a replay window: attachLiveConnection retires\nthe incumbent on redial (correct for a daemon reconnecting after a blip), so a captured 600s token\nEVICTED the real daemon and received that pairing's delegate frames. The chain gains two steps -\n2b spend-the-jti (refuse token-replayed) and 4b token-epoch (refuse token-epoch-stale) - and the\nspend runs BEFORE handleUpgrade, because a refusal after the upgrade has already lost the socket.\nThe ?token= query form is REMOVED (header only): a URL-borne bearer token lands in every proxy log.\n\nADMISSION IS RE-CHECKED WHILE OPEN. A WS is authorised once and then lives for hours, so every\nconnect-time plane silently stopped applying. The heartbeat sweep re-judges activation, billing\nlock and token epoch per live socket and closes with reason 'reauthenticate' ('revoked' stays\nreserved for the terminal tombstone). Worst-case exposure after \"terminar todas as sessoes\" is one\nheartbeat interval instead of the rest of the connection's life.\n\nsecret.deliver is the ONLY frame carrying credential material (bridge/secret-delivery.ts). The\nnonce is REDEEMED BEFORE the unwrap, so a replay causes no decrypt and no second copy on the wire;\na delivery is bound to the pairing it was authorised for; an offline machine is a REFUSAL, never a\nsilent partial. The daemon-side half (RAM only, absent from config.json and the ledger, zeroized\nafter the child exits) is a COUNTERPART obligation - flagged C6/C7 in bridge-counterpart-changes.md\nand tested in that repo. Cortex does not claim to have proven it.\n\nREMAINING (C7): single-use narrows replay from \"the token's full 600s\" to \"a race the attacker must\nwin against the daemon's own dial\". Closing it needs proof-of-possession, which is a two-repo\nchange; J-8 (OS keychain) should land first so the new private key is not written to disk.",
      "originalText": "AS-BUILT 2026-07-28 (J-2/J-3: the connect chain and the one frame that carries a value).\n\nCONNECT is now single-use. A bridge token carries a jti and the connect path SPENDS it exactly\nonce (bridge/connect-nonce.ts). This was not merely a replay window: attachLiveConnection retires\nthe incumbent on redial (correct for a daemon reconnecting after a blip), so a captured 600s token\nEVICTED the real daemon and received that pairing's delegate frames. The chain gains two steps -\n2b spend-the-jti (refuse token-replayed) and 4b token-epoch (refuse token-epoch-stale) - and the\nspend runs BEFORE handleUpgrade, because a refusal after the upgrade has already lost the socket.\nThe ?token= query form is REMOVED (header only): a URL-borne bearer token lands in every proxy log.\n\nADMISSION IS RE-CHECKED WHILE OPEN. A WS is authorised once and then lives for hours, so every\nconnect-time plane silently stopped applying. The heartbeat sweep re-judges activation, billing\nlock and token epoch per live socket and closes with reason 'reauthenticate' ('revoked' stays\nreserved for the terminal tombstone). Worst-case exposure after \"terminar todas as sessoes\" is one\nheartbeat interval instead of the rest of the connection's life.\n\nsecret.deliver is the ONLY frame carrying credential material (bridge/secret-delivery.ts). The\nnonce is REDEEMED BEFORE the unwrap, so a replay causes no decrypt and no second copy on the wire;\na delivery is bound to the pairing it was authorised for; an offline machine is a REFUSAL, never a\nsilent partial. The daemon-side half (RAM only, absent from config.json and the ledger, zeroized\nafter the child exits) is a COUNTERPART obligation - flagged C6/C7 in bridge-counterpart-changes.md\nand tested in that repo. Cortex does not claim to have proven it.\n\nREMAINING (C7): single-use narrows replay from \"the token's full 600s\" to \"a race the attacker must\nwin against the daemon's own dial\". Closing it needs proof-of-possession, which is a two-repo\nchange; J-8 (OS keychain) should land first so the new private key is not written to disk.",
      "fontSize": 14,
      "fontFamily": 3,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "strokeColor": "#0f766e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "angle": 0,
      "seed": 884213771,
      "version": 1,
      "versionNonce": 553120994,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "groupIds": [],
      "boundElements": null,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "containerId": null,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "rawText": "AS-BUILT 2026-07-28 (J-2/J-3: the connect chain and the one frame that carries a value).\n\nCONNECT is now single-use. A bridge token carries a jti and the connect path SPENDS it exactly\nonce (bridge/connect-nonce.ts). This was not merely a replay window: attachLiveConnection retires\nthe incumbent on redial (correct for a daemon reconnecting after a blip), so a captured 600s token\nEVICTED the real daemon and received that pairing's delegate frames. The chain gains two steps -\n2b spend-the-jti (refuse token-replayed) and 4b token-epoch (refuse token-epoch-stale) - and the\nspend runs BEFORE handleUpgrade, because a refusal after the upgrade has already lost the socket.\nThe ?token= query form is REMOVED (header only): a URL-borne bearer token lands in every proxy log.\n\nADMISSION IS RE-CHECKED WHILE OPEN. A WS is authorised once and then lives for hours, so every\nconnect-time plane silently stopped applying. The heartbeat sweep re-judges activation, billing\nlock and token epoch per live socket and closes with reason 'reauthenticate' ('revoked' stays\nreserved for the terminal tombstone). Worst-case exposure after \"terminar todas as sessoes\" is one\nheartbeat interval instead of the rest of the connection's life.\n\nsecret.deliver is the ONLY frame carrying credential material (bridge/secret-delivery.ts). The\nnonce is REDEEMED BEFORE the unwrap, so a replay causes no decrypt and no second copy on the wire;\na delivery is bound to the pairing it was authorised for; an offline machine is a REFUSAL, never a\nsilent partial. The daemon-side half (RAM only, absent from config.json and the ledger, zeroized\nafter the child exits) is a COUNTERPART obligation - flagged C6/C7 in bridge-counterpart-changes.md\nand tested in that repo. Cortex does not claim to have proven it.\n\nREMAINING (C7): single-use narrows replay from \"the token's full 600s\" to \"a race the attacker must\nwin against the daemon's own dial\". Closing it needs proof-of-possession, which is a two-repo\nchange; J-8 (OS keychain) should land first so the new private key is not written to disk."
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "annot-j6j7-20260728",
      "x": 1000,
      "y": 1020,
      "width": 900.0,
      "height": 525.0,
      "text": "AS-BUILT 2026-07-28 (J-6/J-7: the bridge plane gets an audit, and two flags stop being\nself-service).\n\nJ-7 (a). The write confirmation was the MODEL'S to set. The daemon gates a first write on\nconfirmed===true and its header says the user assents Cortex-side; nothing Cortex-side checked it,\nsdk-tools.ts INSTRUCTED the model to set it, and delegateToLocal SIGNS the model's TaskProgram\nverbatim - so Cortex's signature laundered a self-assertion into an authorisation the daemon\ntrusts. Cortex now refuses to SIGN a task whose write step asserts a confirmation with no owner\napproval. Checked before signing; the signature is what makes the assertion powerful. No approval\nUI exists yet, so every model-asserted write is refused today - deliberate.\n\nJ-7 (b). The command shape wildcarded the dangerous part: cat <FILE> matched ~/.ssh/id_rsa and\ncurl -s <URL> matched an attacker host (an APPROVED exfiltration primitive). The bash -c deviation\nhad already made this argument; there is no argument class that generalises safely, so the shape is\nthe exact command. Approvals gained tenant, machine (optional pairingId on the DaemonConnection\nseam) and a 90-day TTL. Legacy wildcard rows are refused, not matched.\n\nJ-6. Durable Registo rows under a `bridge` category for every invocation Cortex can observe:\ndispatch, settlement, refusal, secret delivery, pairing register/revoke. Dispatch and settlement\nare SEPARATE rows - a dispatch with no settlement is a machine that went dark mid-task, and one row\nat the end erases that.\n\nWHAT THE AUDIT MUST NEVER CARRY: paths. EgressLedgerRow carries `path` and 18.2/FC-407 keeps those\nrows un-persisted hosted-side, because a path is itself sensitive (client names in folder names; a\ndirectory listing is privileged for a legal practice). BridgeRegistoMetadata is .strict() with NO\npath field - contract-enforced, not memory-enforced. Tool names come from a closed set; a grep\npattern is text that can embed anything.\n\nCOUNTERPART (flagged, not built): C8 signed per-file approval token instead of a trusted boolean;\nC9 grant issuance/revocation is unaudited on BOTH sides (daemon-side, Cortex never sees it);\nC10 (J-8) credentials still sit in a cleartext config.json - land it BEFORE C7.",
      "originalText": "AS-BUILT 2026-07-28 (J-6/J-7: the bridge plane gets an audit, and two flags stop being\nself-service).\n\nJ-7 (a). The write confirmation was the MODEL'S to set. The daemon gates a first write on\nconfirmed===true and its header says the user assents Cortex-side; nothing Cortex-side checked it,\nsdk-tools.ts INSTRUCTED the model to set it, and delegateToLocal SIGNS the model's TaskProgram\nverbatim - so Cortex's signature laundered a self-assertion into an authorisation the daemon\ntrusts. Cortex now refuses to SIGN a task whose write step asserts a confirmation with no owner\napproval. Checked before signing; the signature is what makes the assertion powerful. No approval\nUI exists yet, so every model-asserted write is refused today - deliberate.\n\nJ-7 (b). The command shape wildcarded the dangerous part: cat <FILE> matched ~/.ssh/id_rsa and\ncurl -s <URL> matched an attacker host (an APPROVED exfiltration primitive). The bash -c deviation\nhad already made this argument; there is no argument class that generalises safely, so the shape is\nthe exact command. Approvals gained tenant, machine (optional pairingId on the DaemonConnection\nseam) and a 90-day TTL. Legacy wildcard rows are refused, not matched.\n\nJ-6. Durable Registo rows under a `bridge` category for every invocation Cortex can observe:\ndispatch, settlement, refusal, secret delivery, pairing register/revoke. Dispatch and settlement\nare SEPARATE rows - a dispatch with no settlement is a machine that went dark mid-task, and one row\nat the end erases that.\n\nWHAT THE AUDIT MUST NEVER CARRY: paths. EgressLedgerRow carries `path` and 18.2/FC-407 keeps those\nrows un-persisted hosted-side, because a path is itself sensitive (client names in folder names; a\ndirectory listing is privileged for a legal practice). BridgeRegistoMetadata is .strict() with NO\npath field - contract-enforced, not memory-enforced. Tool names come from a closed set; a grep\npattern is text that can embed anything.\n\nCOUNTERPART (flagged, not built): C8 signed per-file approval token instead of a trusted boolean;\nC9 grant issuance/revocation is unaudited on BOTH sides (daemon-side, Cortex never sees it);\nC10 (J-8) credentials still sit in a cleartext config.json - land it BEFORE C7.",
      "fontSize": 14,
      "fontFamily": 3,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "strokeColor": "#7c2d12",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "angle": 0,
      "seed": 662114553,
      "version": 1,
      "versionNonce": 771324118,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "groupIds": [],
      "boundElements": null,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "containerId": null,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "rawText": "AS-BUILT 2026-07-28 (J-6/J-7: the bridge plane gets an audit, and two flags stop being\nself-service).\n\nJ-7 (a). The write confirmation was the MODEL'S to set. The daemon gates a first write on\nconfirmed===true and its header says the user assents Cortex-side; nothing Cortex-side checked it,\nsdk-tools.ts INSTRUCTED the model to set it, and delegateToLocal SIGNS the model's TaskProgram\nverbatim - so Cortex's signature laundered a self-assertion into an authorisation the daemon\ntrusts. Cortex now refuses to SIGN a task whose write step asserts a confirmation with no owner\napproval. Checked before signing; the signature is what makes the assertion powerful. No approval\nUI exists yet, so every model-asserted write is refused today - deliberate.\n\nJ-7 (b). The command shape wildcarded the dangerous part: cat <FILE> matched ~/.ssh/id_rsa and\ncurl -s <URL> matched an attacker host (an APPROVED exfiltration primitive). The bash -c deviation\nhad already made this argument; there is no argument class that generalises safely, so the shape is\nthe exact command. Approvals gained tenant, machine (optional pairingId on the DaemonConnection\nseam) and a 90-day TTL. Legacy wildcard rows are refused, not matched.\n\nJ-6. Durable Registo rows under a `bridge` category for every invocation Cortex can observe:\ndispatch, settlement, refusal, secret delivery, pairing register/revoke. Dispatch and settlement\nare SEPARATE rows - a dispatch with no settlement is a machine that went dark mid-task, and one row\nat the end erases that.\n\nWHAT THE AUDIT MUST NEVER CARRY: paths. EgressLedgerRow carries `path` and 18.2/FC-407 keeps those\nrows un-persisted hosted-side, because a path is itself sensitive (client names in folder names; a\ndirectory listing is privileged for a legal practice). BridgeRegistoMetadata is .strict() with NO\npath field - contract-enforced, not memory-enforced. Tool names come from a closed set; a grep\npattern is text that can embed anything.\n\nCOUNTERPART (flagged, not built): C8 signed per-file approval token instead of a trusted boolean;\nC9 grant issuance/revocation is unaudited on BOTH sides (daemon-side, Cortex never sees it);\nC10 (J-8) credentials still sit in a cleartext config.json - land it BEFORE C7."
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "annot-wsefg-20260729",
      "x": 60,
      "y": 1620,
      "width": 900.0,
      "height": 525.0,
      "text": "AS-BUILT 2026-07-29 (WS-E/F/G complete + the daemon seam WIRED).\n\nE-2/E-4. A step now DECLARES where and how it runs (StepDeclaration: requiredCapabilities, target,\nattended, credentialRefs, offlinePolicy) and the router places it on that, not on \"newest live\nsocket for this owner\" \u2014 which meant a step written for the machine with the card reader ran on\nwhatever laptop connected last. Defaults are the CLOSED ones: declaring nothing asks for nothing\nand STOPS (offlinePolicy 'fail'). `cloud` is not a machine; `pinned` is that machine or nothing, no\nsubstitute; `any:<cap>` selects only from machines the ORG GRANTED (I-3). No match returns\nundefined so the CALLER applies the offline policy \u2014 placement reports a fact about the fleet, the\nresponse belongs to the run.\n\nF-3/F-4/F-5. Login recipes are FIXED DATA in api/assets/login-recipes/, loaded through a validator\nthat refuses script-shaped selectors, refuses unrecognised fields rather than ignoring them, and is\nall-or-nothing. A recipe says WHERE the fields are; it never says what to do \u2014 the typist is the\nonly primitive handling a decrypted credential against a live page, so anything steering it is\ninside the credential's trust boundary. `signature` split from `login` (I8): signing is legal\nauthorship bound to the card in the reader, so it routes ATTENDED while a login RELAYS. And a\nCREDENTIAL-ADJACENT failure never reaches the rehearsal fixer \u2014 handing an unfamiliar login form to\nan LLM means its output picks which field receives the password.\n\nG-4/G-5. Checkout asks health FIRST, then egress. An expired/unhealthy session returns a\nRE-ESTABLISHMENT ROUTE chosen by provenance (card ceremony -> attended, password -> typist, unknown\n-> attended, the conservative direction). A residential-bound session refuses checkout when only\ndatacenter egress is available: same cookie, different continent is the pattern fraud systems flag,\nand another machine's residential line is not a substitute.\n\nTHE SEAM IS WIRED. setDaemonConnectionResolver at server.ts now returns a live connection, so\nlocal_command and daemon browser steps are reachable end to end. The plan calls this \"the single\nmost dangerous ordering mistake available\" and gates it behind H-1, H-4, J-4, J-7 \u2014 all in. The\nproperty that makes it safe: wiring grants NOTHING. invokeTool refuses before sending unless the ORG\ngranted that capability on that machine, re-read PER INVOCATION so a mid-run revoke stops the next\nstep. tool.invoke/tool.result gained the awaiting coordinator they lacked; every invocation settles\n(refused / unreachable / timeout / disconnect), so a run never hangs on a machine.",
      "originalText": "AS-BUILT 2026-07-29 (WS-E/F/G complete + the daemon seam WIRED).\n\nE-2/E-4. A step now DECLARES where and how it runs (StepDeclaration: requiredCapabilities, target,\nattended, credentialRefs, offlinePolicy) and the router places it on that, not on \"newest live\nsocket for this owner\" \u2014 which meant a step written for the machine with the card reader ran on\nwhatever laptop connected last. Defaults are the CLOSED ones: declaring nothing asks for nothing\nand STOPS (offlinePolicy 'fail'). `cloud` is not a machine; `pinned` is that machine or nothing, no\nsubstitute; `any:<cap>` selects only from machines the ORG GRANTED (I-3). No match returns\nundefined so the CALLER applies the offline policy \u2014 placement reports a fact about the fleet, the\nresponse belongs to the run.\n\nF-3/F-4/F-5. Login recipes are FIXED DATA in api/assets/login-recipes/, loaded through a validator\nthat refuses script-shaped selectors, refuses unrecognised fields rather than ignoring them, and is\nall-or-nothing. A recipe says WHERE the fields are; it never says what to do \u2014 the typist is the\nonly primitive handling a decrypted credential against a live page, so anything steering it is\ninside the credential's trust boundary. `signature` split from `login` (I8): signing is legal\nauthorship bound to the card in the reader, so it routes ATTENDED while a login RELAYS. And a\nCREDENTIAL-ADJACENT failure never reaches the rehearsal fixer \u2014 handing an unfamiliar login form to\nan LLM means its output picks which field receives the password.\n\nG-4/G-5. Checkout asks health FIRST, then egress. An expired/unhealthy session returns a\nRE-ESTABLISHMENT ROUTE chosen by provenance (card ceremony -> attended, password -> typist, unknown\n-> attended, the conservative direction). A residential-bound session refuses checkout when only\ndatacenter egress is available: same cookie, different continent is the pattern fraud systems flag,\nand another machine's residential line is not a substitute.\n\nTHE SEAM IS WIRED. setDaemonConnectionResolver at server.ts now returns a live connection, so\nlocal_command and daemon browser steps are reachable end to end. The plan calls this \"the single\nmost dangerous ordering mistake available\" and gates it behind H-1, H-4, J-4, J-7 \u2014 all in. The\nproperty that makes it safe: wiring grants NOTHING. invokeTool refuses before sending unless the ORG\ngranted that capability on that machine, re-read PER INVOCATION so a mid-run revoke stops the next\nstep. tool.invoke/tool.result gained the awaiting coordinator they lacked; every invocation settles\n(refused / unreachable / timeout / disconnect), so a run never hangs on a machine.",
